'''Richard Paul Astley''' (born 6 February 1966) is an English singer, songwriter, radio DJ and podcaster.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Rick Astley to join Greatest Hits Radio for special New Year's Eve show|url=https://planetradio.co.uk/greatest-hits/station/on-air/rick-astley-new-years-eve/|access-date=2022-01-05|website=Greatest Hits Radio|language=en|archive-date=21 May 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240521124507/https://planetradio.co.uk/greatest-hits/station/on-air/rick-astley-new-years-eve/|url-status=live}}</ref> He gained fame through his association with the production trio [[Stock Aitken Waterman]], releasing the 1987 album ''[[Whenever You Need Somebody]]'', which sold 15 million copies worldwide and was certified platinum by both the [[British Phonographic Industry|BPI]] and the [[Recording Industry Association of America|RIAA]]. His debut single "[[Never Gonna Give You Up]]" was a No. 1 hit in more than 25 countries, winning the [[8th Brit Awards|1988 Brit Award]] for [[Brit Award for British Single|Best British Single]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.mikestockmusic.com/index.php?page=Biography02&section=sectionBiography |title=Biography |publisher=Mike Stock Music |access-date=11 June 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111003132814/http://www.mikestockmusic.com/index.php?page=Biography02&section=sectionBiography |archive-date=3 October 2011 |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref name="RickBio">{{cite web |url=http://www.rickastley.co.uk/biography/biography.html |title=Biography |website=Rickastley.co.uk |access-date=12 December 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100210092804/http://www.rickastley.co.uk/biography/biography.html |archive-date=10 February 2010}}</ref> The song also stayed [[List of UK Singles Chart number ones|at the top of the UK chart]] for five weeks in 1987 and was the best-selling single of that year. His 1988 single "[[Together Forever (Rick Astley song)|Together Forever]]" also topped the US [[Billboard Hot 100|''Billboard'' Hot 100]] and was one of his eight songs to reach the [[UK Singles Chart]] top 10.

In 1988, Astley followed up his debut album with ''[[Hold Me in Your Arms (album)|Hold Me in Your Arms]]'' which achieved platinum and gold certifications internationally. The lead single "[[She Wants to Dance with Me]]" became a worldwide top 10 hit. In 1991, he left Stock Aitken Waterman and moved his musical direction towards [[soul music|soul]], which he explored on his other albums. His 1991 single "[[Cry for Help]]" was his last single to reach the top 10 in either the US or UK. In 1993, he retired from music to focus on spending time with his partner and daughter. He returned to the music industry in 2000 and released ''[[50 (Rick Astley album)|50]]'' to celebrate his 50th birthday in 2016, with the album debuting in the UK at No.&nbsp;1.<ref name="50no1" /> His most recent studio album ''[[Are We There Yet? (Rick Astley album)|Are We There Yet?]]'' was released in 2023.<ref name="tone">{{Cite web|date=2 June 2020|title=Rick Astley's best songs that aren't 'Never Gonna Give You Up'|url=https://tonedeaf.thebrag.com/rick-astleys-best-songs-that-arent-never-gonna-give-you-up/|access-date=7 September 2021|website=Tone Deaf|language=en-AU|archive-date=21 May 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240521124452/https://tonedeaf.thebrag.com/rick-astleys-best-songs-that-arent-never-gonna-give-you-up/|url-status=live}}</ref>

Astley has sold approximately 40 million records worldwide and was nominated in 1989 for a [[Grammy Award]] for Best New Artist but lost to [[Tracy Chapman]].<ref>[http://www.shropshirestar.com/2008/06/07/ricks-rolling-back-the-years/ Shropshire Star.com – 7 June 2008] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080610163403/http://www.shropshirestar.com/2008/06/07/ricks-rolling-back-the-years/|date=10 June 2008}}. Retrieved 28 January 2010.</ref><ref>[http://www.liverpoolecho.co.uk/liverpool-news/local-news/2007/11/12/madonna-must-be-made-of-steel-but-i-was-too-sensitive-for-stardom-100252-20097466/ Liverpool Echo – 12 November 2007] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081205074602/http://www.liverpoolecho.co.uk/liverpool-news/local-news/2007/11/12/madonna-must-be-made-of-steel-but-i-was-too-sensitive-for-stardom-100252-20097466/|date=5 December 2008}}. Retrieved 28 January 2010.</ref> During the period between his debut release and his fifth single, Astley outsold every other artist in the world.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.sundaysun.co.uk/news/your-questions/2008/02/24/ask-rick-astley-79310-20515633/|title=Ask Rick Astley|publisher=Sunday Sun|access-date=15 May 2010|archive-date=13 September 2012|archive-url=https://archive.today/20120913214518/http://www.sundaysun.co.uk/news/your-questions/2008/02/24/ask-rick-astley-79310-20515633/|url-status=live}}</ref> In the United Kingdom, Astley was also in the top 40 every week for the first six months of his career.<ref name="RickBio"/> He became an [[Internet meme|internet phenomenon]] in 2007 following the emergence of the [[Rickrolling]] meme, which utilizes the music video for "Never Gonna Give You Up",<ref>[http://www.vh1la.com/bands/az/astley_rick/bio.jhtml Rick Biografia] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090521113946/http://www.vh1la.com/bands/az/astley_rick/bio.jhtml |date=21 May 2009 }}. VH1 Spañol (undated). Retrieved 27 December 2006.</ref> and his career was revitalised by the meme's popularity. A year after the Rickrolling meme began, he was voted "Best Act Ever" at the [[MTV Europe Music Awards 2008]].<ref name="bbc1">{{cite news|date=2 October 2008|title=Astley shortlisted for MTV award|work=BBC News|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/entertainment/7646807.stm|url-status=live|access-date=22 November 2008|archive-url=https://archive.today/20120718155106/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/entertainment/7646807.stm|archive-date=18 July 2012}}</ref> On 28 July 2021, "Never Gonna Give You Up" became the fourth 1980s [[List of most-viewed YouTube videos|song to reach one billion views]] on [[YouTube]].{{Efn|Behind "[[Billie Jean]]" by [[Michael Jackson]], "[[Take On Me]]" by [[A-ha]] and "[[Sweet Child o' Mine]]" by [[Guns N' Roses]].<ref>{{Cite web|last=Spangler|first=Todd|date=29 July 2021|title=Rick Astley's 'Never Gonna Give You Up' Rolls Past 1 Billion YouTube Views|url=https://variety.com/2021/digital/news/rick-astley-never-gonna-give-you-up-1-billion-youtube-views-1235030404/|access-date=6 September 2021|website=Variety|language=en-US|archive-date=28 December 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211228091708/https://variety.com/2021/digital/news/rick-astley-never-gonna-give-you-up-1-billion-youtube-views-1235030404/|url-status=live}}</ref>}}

==Early life==
Richard Paul Astley was born on 6 February 1966 in [[Newton-le-Willows]], [[Lancashire]], the fourth child of Horace and Cynthia Astley. His parents divorced when he was 5 and Astley was brought up by his father who was "really, really pissed off a lot of the time... but he was great as well, he was really loving".<ref name="tone"/><ref name="Independent">{{cite news|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/people/profiles/rick-astley-the-pop-idol-returns-319249.html|title=Rick Astley: The pop idol returns|newspaper=[[The Independent]]|access-date=16 May 2010|location=London|date=13 October 2005|archive-date=5 June 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160605091222/http://www.independent.co.uk/news/people/profiles/rick-astley-the-pop-idol-returns-319249.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Rick remained in very close contact with his mother, who lived a few streets away.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.express.co.uk/life-style/life/978496/Rick-Astley-embarrassed-millionaire-22|title=I was embarrassed being a millionaire at just 22|last=Barber|first=Richard|date=23 June 2018|work=Daily Express|access-date=3 September 2018|archive-date=4 September 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180904052521/https://www.express.co.uk/life-style/life/978496/Rick-Astley-embarrassed-millionaire-22|url-status=live}}</ref> Describing his childhood, Astley stated "There was definitely an element of 'There's something very wrong in this family.' I can't even say I've got these exact memories of it, I just know it was a bit odd."<ref name="tone"/>

Astley would also later recall that his worst childhood memory was "Walking in the kitchen and seeing my dad with his hands around my mum's neck. After something like that, things get broken and destroyed in your emotional and mental make-up. It was not Walt Disney; it was not what's supposed to happen. Shouting and arguing is bad enough, but for me something [broke] that day. Everything about it terrified me and just completely flipped me out. No kid should ever see that."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Aubrey|first=Liz|date=2024-10-26|title=Rick Astley: I have a sense of 'thank Christ' for that song |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/music/interviews/rick-astley-interview-never-gonna-give-you-up/ |website=www.telegraph.co.uk|language=en-GB}}</ref> In an interview ahead of the publication of his autobiography, Astley said "I think it did form me as a person, definitely, and formed a lot of my choices of why I went into music and why I wanted to be on a stage and why I wanted people's attention – because I don't think I got enough attention from my parents."<ref>{{Cite web |date=8 October 2024|title=Family heartbreak put Rick Astley on path to fame|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c8xey91g49eo|website=www.bbc.co.uk|language=en-GB}}</ref>

When he was 10, Astley began singing in a local church choir, which began his love of music.<ref name="LookStudio">{{cite web|url=http://lookstudio.com/rickastley/about.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20020205030445/http://www.lookstudio.com/rickastley/about.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=5 February 2002 |title=Rick Astley – About |website=Lookstudio.com |access-date=16 May 2010 }}</ref> During his schooldays, Astley played the drums in a number of local bands, where he met guitarist [[David Morris (Conservative politician)|David Morris]].<ref name="RickBio" /><ref name="BBC12523570" /> After leaving school at 16, Astley was employed during the day as a driver in his father's market-gardening business and played drums on the [[Northern club circuit]] at night in bands such as Give Way – specialising in covering [[Beatles]] and [[The Shadows|Shadows]] songs – and FBI, which won several local talent competitions.<ref name="LookStudio" />

==Career==
===1985–1986: Signing with Stock Aitken Waterman===
In 1985, Astley was performing as a drummer for the soul band FBI, with Morris on guitar. They were a well-known local band, gigging in pubs and clubs. When FBI's lead singer left the band and Morris left to concentrate on his career in [[hairdressing]],<ref name=BBC12523570/> Astley offered to be the lead vocalist. That was when he was noticed by the record producer [[Pete Waterman]] at a [[Warrington]] nightclub, who offered him the opportunity to come to [[London]] to work at the [[Pete Waterman Entertainment|Pete Waterman Limited]] (PWL) recording studio with [[RCA Records]] publishing his records.<ref name="RickBio"/> Although Astley initially turned down Waterman's proposal, several months later he accepted the offer and moved into Waterman's [[London]] flat.<ref name=making>{{cite web|url=https://www.classicpopmag.com/2021/07/rick-astley-whenever-you-need-somebody/|title=Making Rick Astley: Whenever You Need Somebody|website=Classic Pop|date=5 July 2021 |access-date=August 9, 2022}}</ref> SAW also hired most of FBI, including Morris as a guitarist/songwriter.<ref name=BBC12523570>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-politics-12523570|title=MP David Morris on his journey from pop to politics|publisher=BBC|access-date=2 March 2011|date=2 March 2011|archive-date=15 June 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180615141759/https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-politics-12523570|url-status=live}}</ref>

Under the tutelage of the production team of [[Mike Stock (musician)|Mike Stock]], [[Matt Aitken]], and Waterman, known as [[Stock Aitken Waterman]] (SAW), Astley was taught about the [[sound recording and reproduction|recording]] process and groomed for his future career, starting off as the recording studio "tea boy", an apprenticeship that lasted two years. The reason for Astley to be hired as a "tape op" was to overcome his shyness.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Y9fNRXUoveg|title=YouTube – Rick Astley Interview in 1987 (TVam) (1987)|date=2 October 2008 |via=YouTube|access-date=14 May 2010|archive-date=12 February 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130212081017/http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Y9fNRXUoveg&feature=related|url-status=live}}</ref> Reflecting on this time, Astley said: "I was seeing other people have No. 1 hits, and I'd think: 'Come on, boys'. But it wasn't that they had forgotten me, they just had a lot of records to make. I knew, though, that when I got my turn, it would be a big thing, because Stock Aitken Waterman were becoming this big hit machine."<ref name=making/>

===1987–1989: Success===
In March 1987, Astley contributed vocals to "[[Let It Be (song)|Let It Be]]" which was recorded by [[charity supergroup]] [[Ferry Aid]]. The single topped the [[UK Singles Chart]] for three weeks <ref name=ukchart>{{cite web |url=https://www.officialcharts.com/search/singles/I%20Know%20You%20Got%20Soul |title=The Official Charts Company - Ferry Aid - Let It Be |website=[[OfficialCharts.com]] |access-date=5 July 2009}}</ref> and was certified gold for shipping over 500,000 copies.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.bpi.co.uk/certifiedawards/search.aspx |title=Certified Awards Search |publisher=[[British Phonographic Industry|BPI]] |access-date=26 November 2011 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://archive.today/20090901062056/http://www.bpi.co.uk/certifiedawards/search.aspx |archive-date=1 September 2009 |df=dmy }} Search by Title, click on more info>></ref><ref>{{cite web
  | title = Certified Awards – A Timeline
  | work = Certified Awards Timeline.pdf
  | publisher = BPI (British Recorded Music Industry) Limited
  | location = London, UK
  | url = http://www.bpi.co.uk/assets/files/Certified%20Awards%20Timeline.pdf
  | access-date = 27 November 2011
  | archive-date = 7 August 2011
  | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20110807030054/http://www.bpi.co.uk/assets/files/Certified%20Awards%20Timeline.pdf
  | url-status = dead
  }}</ref> It was also a No. 1 hit in [[Norway]] and [[Switzerland]].<ref name=norway>{{cite web|url=http://norwegiancharts.com/showitem.asp?interpret=Ferry+Aid&titel=Let+It+Be&cat=s |title=norwegiancharts.com - Ferry Aid - Let It Be |access-date=5 July 2009 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110515082401/http://norwegiancharts.com/showitem.asp?interpret=Ferry%2BAid&titel=Let%2BIt%2BBe&cat=s |archive-date=15 May 2011 |df=dmy }}</ref><ref name=switzerland>{{cite web|url=http://hitparade.ch/showitem.asp?interpret=Ferry+Aid&titel=Let+It+Be&cat=s |title=Ferry Aid - Let It Be - hitparade.ch |language=de |access-date=5 July 2009 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110516023409/http://hitparade.ch/showitem.asp?interpret=Ferry%2BAid&titel=Let%2BIt%2BBe&cat=s |archive-date=16 May 2011 |df=dmy }}</ref> Astley's first single was "[[Greatest Hits (Rick Astley album)#"When You Gonna"|When You Gonna]]", released as a duet between Astley and singer Lisa Fabien; the song was produced by [[Phil Harding (producer)|Phil Harding]] and Ian Curnow. It was released in May 1987, and with no promotion managed to chart at No. 17 in [[the Netherlands]] and No. 20 in [[Belgium]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://dutchcharts.nl/showitem.asp?interpret=Rick+%26+Lisa&titel=When+You+Gonna&cat=s|title=Rick & Lisa - When You Gonna - Dutch Charts|website=dutchcharts.nl}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.ultratop.be/nl/song/6dae/rick-&-lisa-when-you-gonna|title=Rick & Lisa - When You Gonna|website=ultratop.be}}</ref> It also charted on the US ''Billboard'' Dance Club Songs chart at No. 11.<ref>{{Cite magazine|title=Rick And Lisa|url=https://www.billboard.com/artist/rick-and-lisa/chart-history/dsi/|access-date=2021-11-16|magazine=Billboard}}</ref>

"We were at a stage early on with Rick, when no one knew quite what to do with him," recalled Harding. "The idea was to make him happen in the clubs... but the song opened up the doors for him at [[Capital London|Capital Radio]]."<ref>{{Cite web|title=A Journey Through Stock Aitken Waterman: Ep 21: A Detour With Harding & Curnow Part 2 on Apple Podcasts|url=https://podcasts.apple.com/au/podcast/ep-21-a-detour-with-harding-curnow-part-2/id1565879477?i=1000548034733|access-date=2022-01-17|website=Apple Podcasts|language=en-AU|archive-date=21 May 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240521124416/https://podcasts.apple.com/au/podcast/ep-21-a-detour-with-harding-curnow-part-2/id1565879477?i=1000548034733|url-status=live}}</ref> Astley had also contributed to another duet with [[O'Chi Brown]], "[[Learning to Live (Without Your Love)]]" for her 1986 album, ''O'Chi''. Astley was uncredited on the album and received no royalties. The song was belatedly released as a cash-in single in October 1987. The song failed to chart on the [[UK Singles Chart]]. The two singers never met, and Brown first heard Astley's vocal when she was given a copy of her album.<ref name="Scott-2022" />

Astley's first solo offering was "[[Never Gonna Give You Up]]", recorded on New Year's Day 1987, and released nearly seven months later, on 27 July.<ref name="NGGYU">{{cite web|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3HrSN7176XI |title=YouTube – Rick Astley – Never Gonna Give You Up (1987) |via=YouTube|access-date=11 February 2010 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100212020403/http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3HrSN7176XI |archive-date=12 February 2010 }}</ref> "At first, Pete felt we should do a [[Motown]] cover," said [[Mike Stock (musician)|Mike Stock]]. "The first thing we recorded was [[The Temptations]]' "[[Ain't Too Proud to Beg]]" and that's when I first properly heard his voice. I said to Pete and Matt that singing a cover would be to underplay and undervalue his undoubted vocal strengths, and that we should write Rick his own song. That's when we came up with [[Never Gonna Give You Up]]."<ref name=making/> The concept for the song was suggested by Waterman after Astley spoke to him of his devotion to his then girlfriend, with the song's tune, chords and lyrics composed by Stock and [[Matt Aitken]].<ref name="JourneyStockPodcast-27">{{Cite web |title=A Journey Through Stock Aitken Waterman: Ep 27: Never Gonna Give You Up on Apple Podcasts |url=https://podcasts.apple.com/au/podcast/ep-27-never-gonna-give-you-up/id1565879477?i=1000556095254 |access-date=2022-04-05 |website=Apple Podcasts |language=en-AU |archive-date=21 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240521124428/https://podcasts.apple.com/au/podcast/ep-27-never-gonna-give-you-up/id1565879477?i=1000556095254 |url-status=live }}</ref>

[[Mike Stock (musician)|Mike Stock]] stated that the [[Colonel Abrams]] hit "[[Trapped (Colonel Abrams song)|Trapped]]" (1985) was a big influence on "Never Gonna Give You Up", saying: "For Rick Astley's song I didn't want it to sound like [[Kylie Minogue|Kylie]] or [[Bananarama]] so I looked at the Colonel Abrams track 'Trapped' and recreated that syncopated bassline in a way that suited our song."<ref>{{cite news|first= Tim |last= Jonze |title= 'I crawled on my knees to Kylie' – the inside story of Stock, Aitken and Waterman |newspaper= [[The Guardian]] |date= 28 March 2017 |access-date= 3 September 2020 |url= https://www.theguardian.com/music/musicblog/2017/mar/28/stock-aitken-waterman-mike-hit-factory-pop-songwriters-kylie |archive-date= 24 September 2020 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20200924150603/https://www.theguardian.com/music/musicblog/2017/mar/28/stock-aitken-waterman-mike-hit-factory-pop-songwriters-kylie |url-status= live}}</ref> Initial mixing was done by [[Phil Harding (producer)|Phil Harding]], with the song's distinctive synthetic string and brass lines later added by [[Fairlight CMI|Fairlight]] operator Ian Curnow.<ref name="JourneyStockPodcast-27" /> The final mix was provided by [[Pete Waterman Entertainment|PWL]] remixer Pete Hammond, who made the vocals more prominent. His completed extended mix was edited down by Stock and Aitken to become the radio version.<ref name="JourneyStockPodcast-27" />

Although his producers were initially unenthusiastic about the track,<ref name="JourneyStockPodcast-27"/> his distinctive rich, deep voice combined with dance-pop made the song an immediate success,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.allmusic.com/artist/p3581|title=allmusic – Rick Astley – Overview|publisher=[[AllMusic]]|access-date=14 May 2010|archive-date=15 January 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120115040108/http://www.allmusic.com/artist/p3581|url-status=live}}</ref> spending five weeks at the top of the [[The Official UK Charts Company|British charts]] and becoming the year's highest-selling single. The song was also a worldwide No. 1 hit, topping the charts in 24 other countries, including the [[United States]], [[Australia]], and [[West Germany]]. The single reached the No. 1 spot on the year-end singles charts in both the [[United Kingdom]] and [[South Africa]]. "Never Gonna Give You Up" won Best British Single at the 1988 BPI Awards (now called the [[BRIT Awards]]),<ref name="talktalk" /> and Astley performed it in front of a global audience of 100 million.<ref name="RickBio" /><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YnM1Ee_H2As |title=YouTube – Rick Astley – Never Gonna Give You Up (live) 1988 |via=[[YouTube]]|access-date=17 May 2010 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110803233913/http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YnM1Ee_H2As&gl=US&hl=en&amp;has_verified=1 |archive-date=3 August 2011 }}</ref> Assessing the status of the track as producers [[Stock Aitken Waterman]]'s biggest and most enduring hit, Stock confessed he struggled to completely understand why the song had struck such a chord, but suggested its massive success was down to a combination of the song, the singer, and the international clout of record label [[RCA Records|RCA]].<ref name="JourneyStockPodcast-27" />

Astley's next single was "[[Whenever You Need Somebody (song)|Whenever You Need Somebody]]", which was released in October 1987. The single was a recycled Stock Aitken Waterman song, originally recorded by [[O'Chi Brown]] in 1985, which Stock said was resurrected in the rush to gather strong follow up material in the wake of Astley's unexpectedly huge global success.<ref name="Scott-2022">{{Cite podcast |url=https://podcasts.apple.com/au/podcast/ep-29-learning-to-live-without-your-love-to-turn-it-up/id1565879477?i=1000559977531 |title=Ep 29: Learning To Live (Without Your Love) to Turn It Up |website=A Journey Through Stock Aitken Waterman |date=2022-05-08 |via=[[Apple Podcasts]] |last=Scott |first=Gavin |last2=Denby |first2=Matthew}}</ref> Astley's version of "Whenever You Need Somebody" was a major hit on the charts--a Top 5 hit in almost all the countries where it was released. It became successful in Europe, reaching No. 1 in seven countries, including West Germany and [[Sweden]]. It also reached No. 3 in the [[United Kingdom]], a position it held for three weeks. Though not released as a single in [[America]], it was released in [[Canada]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.rickastley.co.uk/discography/singles/singles02.html |title=Discography – Whenever You Need Somebody |website=Rickastley.co.uk |access-date=15 May 2010 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100413124135/http://www.rickastley.co.uk/discography/singles/singles02.html |archive-date=13 April 2010 }}</ref> Discussing Astley's success with the track, Brown complimented the singer on his voice and praised the chart performance of his cover.<ref>{{Cite web|title=A Journey Through Stock Aitken Waterman: Ep 12: Tell Me Tomorrow to New York Afternoon on Apple Podcasts|url=https://podcasts.apple.com/au/podcast/ep-12-tell-me-tomorrow-to-new-york-afternoon/id1565879477?i=1000535927660|access-date=2021-09-21|website=Apple Podcasts|language=en-AU}}</ref>

In November 1987, the album ''[[Whenever You Need Somebody]]'', containing four tracks written by Astley, reached No. 1 in both [[United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland|Britain]] and [[Australia]] and No. 10 in [[America]]. It was certified 4× Platinum in England and [[Canada]], and 2× Platinum in [[America]]. It is Astley's most best-selling album and it sold 15.2 million copies worldwide, making Astley the top-selling [[British people|British]] act of the year.<ref name="RickBio"/> The album is listed as the 136th best-selling album in Spain,<ref>[http://www.albertocomesana.es/viewtopic.php?t=248 Untitled]. ''albertocomesana.es''. Retrieved 8 January 2010. {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110720133230/http://www.albertocomesana.es/viewtopic.php?t=248 |date=20 July 2011 }}</ref> and was the seventh best-selling album of 1987 in the United Kingdom.<ref name="UK YEC">{{cite news |url=https://worldradiohistory.com/UK/Record-Mirror/80s/88/Record-Mirror-1988-01-23-OCR.pdf |title=Gallup Year End Charts 1987 – Albums |newspaper=[[Record Mirror]] |date=23 January 1988 |page=37<!-- 36 in PDF file --> |issn=0144-5804 |via=World Radio History}}</ref> Alex Henderson of [[AllMusic]] stated that SAW "should definitely be proud of their work" on this "often captivating debut album" which "proved [the singer] to be a welcome addition to the British R&B scene"; he added that the "music approach is very much a production of the '70s" and highlighted tracks like "Together Forever", "Never Gonna Give You Up" and "Don't Say Goodbye".<ref name="allmusic">{{cite web|last=Henderson|first=Alex|url=https://www.allmusic.com/album/whenever-you-need-somebody-mw0000194422|title=Whenever You Need Somebody – Rick Astley|website=[[AllMusic]]|access-date=20 November 2019}}</ref>

In December 1987, Astley released a cover version of the [[Nat King Cole]] classic "[[When I Fall in Love]]". Rivals [[EMI]], hoping to see their act the [[Pet Shop Boys]] become UK [[Christmas Number 1|Christmas No. 1]], re-released Cole's version.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://itunes.apple.com/gb/album/take-me-to-your-heart/id277825763?i=277825791&ign-mpt=uo%3D4|title=The Ultimate Collection by Rick Astley|publisher=iTunes Store|access-date=15 May 2010|archive-date=10 May 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110510023030/http://itunes.apple.com/gb/album/take-me-to-your-heart/id277825763?i=277825791&ign-mpt=uo%3D4|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.rickastley.co.uk/discography/singles/singles03.html |title=Discography – When I Fall in Love |website=Rickastley.co.uk |access-date=15 May 2010 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100419231419/http://www.rickastley.co.uk/discography/singles/singles03.html |archive-date=19 April 2010 }}</ref> That led to a slowdown of purchases of Astley's version, allowing the Pet Shop Boys to reach the top spot with their cover version of "[[Always on My Mind]]". "When I Fall in Love" sold over 200,000 copies and gained a Silver certification from the BPI;<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.bpi.co.uk/certifiedawards/search.aspx |title=Certified Awards Search |publisher=BPI |access-date=15 May 2010 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090924015932/http://www.bpi.co.uk/certifiedawards/Search.aspx |archive-date=24 September 2009 }}</ref> it also peaked in the UK at No. 2 for two weeks. The second half of the [[A-side and B-side|double A-side]] single was a dance number, "[[My Arms Keep Missing You]]", which was released as an independent single in [[Germany]] and reached No. 6.

Astley's fourth single release was "[[Together Forever (Rick Astley song)|Together Forever]]" in January 1988, reaching No. 2 in the UK. It was denied the top spot by [[Kylie Minogue]]'s debut "[[I Should Be So Lucky]]", which reached No. 1 following her successful role as [[Charlene Robinson]] in ''[[Neighbours]]''.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.officialcharts.com/charts/|title=Singles Chart 12/03/88|publisher=[[Official Charts Company]]|access-date=15 May 2010|archive-date=26 May 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200526094853/https://www.officialcharts.com/charts//|url-status=live}}</ref> "Together Forever" was more successful in [[America]], topping the charts. It also topped the charts in Canada, Ireland and Spain, and was a Top 10 hit in many European countries. His fifth and final release from his debut album was "[[It Would Take a Strong Strong Man]]". It was mainly intended for the American market and was not released in the United Kingdom and most of Europe. It was another hit, reaching No. 10 on the US ''Billboard Hot 100'' and No. 1 in Canada. The B-side "You Move Me" written by Astley was featured in the movie ''[[Cocktail (1988 film)|Cocktail]]''.<ref>[https://twitter.com/rickastley/status/1528820772977917953 Tim's Twitter Listening Party]</ref>

Production of the singer's follow up album was complicated when a distressed Astley rejected the set's intended first single, "Nothing Can Divide Us"—a track the producers then gave to [[Jason Donovan]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=A Journey Through Stock Aitken Waterman: Ep 36: The Loco-Motion to Turn It Into Love on Apple Podcasts |url=https://podcasts.apple.com/au/podcast/ep-36-the-loco-motion-to-turn-it-into-love/id1565879477?i=1000576045102 |access-date=2022-10-01 |website=Apple Podcasts |language=en-AU |archive-date=15 August 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220815094317/https://podcasts.apple.com/au/podcast/ep-36-the-loco-motion-to-turn-it-into-love/id1565879477?i=1000576045102 |url-status=live }}</ref> Wishing to placate an unhappy Astley, producer [[Pete Waterman]] allowed him to start work with other writers and producers under the PWL umbrella,<ref name="JourneyStockPodcast-39">{{Cite web |title=A Journey Through Stock Aitken Waterman: Ep 39: Success to S.S. Paparazzi on Apple Podcasts |url=https://podcasts.apple.com/au/podcast/ep-39-success-to-s-s-paparazzi/id1565879477?i=1000580576696 |access-date=2022-10-01 |website=Apple Podcasts |language=en-AU |archive-date=21 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240521125538/https://podcasts.apple.com/au/podcast/ep-39-success-to-s-s-paparazzi/id1565879477?i=1000580576696 |url-status=live }}</ref> but a fire in the PWL studios destroyed much of Astley's new material,<ref name="RickBio"/> causing a delay in the release of his second album.<ref name="JourneyStockPodcast-39"/> ''[[Hold Me in Your Arms (Rick Astley album)|Hold Me in Your Arms]]'' was released in January 1989. It contained five singles and reached No. 8 in the UK and No. 19 in the US, achieving Platinum and Gold certifications worldwide. ''[[Number One (magazine)|Number One]]'' gave a very enthusiastic review of the album: "We say hoorah! Some pundits say this sounds exactly like Rick's last disc, but this isn't a bad thing. It just means there's more swirling singalongs".<ref name="NumberOneReview">{{cite magazine|last=|first=|url=https://api.flickr.com/photos/57779449@N02/50342582102/in/album-72157715970149126/|title=Review: Rick Astley — ''Hold Me In Your Arms'' (RCA)|magazine=[[Number One (magazine)|Number One]]|date=14 December 1988|page=36|issue=287|location=London|publisher=IPC Magazines Ltd.|issn=}}</ref> Astley's relationship with [[British people|British]] media deteriorated significantly after the release of ''Hold Me in Your Arms'', with the media calling him a "puppet" of Stock Aitken Waterman,<ref name="talktalk">{{cite web|url=http://www.talktalk.co.uk/music/biography/artist/rick-astley/biography/10843|title=Rick Astley – Biography|publisher=TalkTalk|access-date=15 May 2010 }}{{dead link|date=December 2012}}</ref> although Astley had written five of his new album's tracks. The negative press affected the sales of his singles.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.rickastley.co.uk/discography/singles/singles09.html |title=Discography -Hold Me in Your Arms |website=Rickastley.co.uk |access-date=15 May 2010 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100727071046/http://www.rickastley.co.uk/discography/singles/singles09.html |archive-date=27 July 2010 }}</ref>

The first single from the album was "[[She Wants to Dance with Me]]", written by Astley. He composed the track in the style of [[Whitney Houston]]'s hit, "[[I Wanna Dance with Somebody (Who Loves Me)|I Wanna Dance With Somebody (Who Loves Me)]]" in an effort to win the confidence of Waterman, who was a fan of Houston's song.<ref name="JourneyStockPodcast-39" /> It was another international success, peaking in the Top 10 in many countries, including Canada, where it topped the chart, and the US and the UK, where it reached No. 6. "[[Take Me to Your Heart (Rick Astley song)|Take Me to Your Heart]]" was the next single to be released from the album. It was a success on the [[UK Singles Chart]] where it peaked at No. 8 and it was also a Top 10 and 20 hit in many other European countries. It was one of four songs written and produced by Stock Aitken Waterman that were belatedly added to the album in order to help Astley make his release deadline following the fire.<ref name="JourneyStockPodcast-39" /> The four tracks were written and produced in just two days, with writer [[Matt Aitken]] confirming "Take Me to Your Heart" was inspired by [[house music]] act [[Inner City (band)|Inner City]]'s 1988 hit, "[[Big Fun (Inner City song)|Big Fun]]".<ref name="JourneyStockPodcast-39" />

"[[Hold Me in Your Arms (Rick Astley song)|Hold Me in Your Arms]]", a ballad written by Astley, reached No. 10 in the UK and was also not released in North America. The next two singles released from the album were intended for the North American market. "[[Giving Up on Love]]" and a cover of [[The Temptations]] song "[[Ain't Too Proud to Beg]]" charted at No. 38 and No. 89 in the US respectively. In December 1989, Astley set off on his first world tour, touring 15 countries including the UK, US, Australia, and Japan.<ref name="RickBio"/> By the end of the tour, he was tired of the negative press and wanted to explore alternative paths as a musician. He left Stock Aitken Waterman and [[RCA Records]] bought out his contract with PWL.

===1990–1993: Switch to adult contemporary and career break===
By 1990, Astley decided to leave dance-pop and move towards soul. The shift in musical genre led him to change his image, ditching the [[Girl next door|boy next door]] look, growing his hair, and presenting himself as a mature and passionate musician. His third album, ''[[Free (Rick Astley album)|Free]]'', was released in 1991 and contained collaborations with [[Elton John]].<ref name="talktalk"/> It had three singles and reached No. 9 in the UK and No. 31 in the US. Astley achieved one more major success with his 1991 ballad "[[Cry for Help]]", which reached No. 7 on both the [[UK Singles Chart]] and the US [[Billboard Hot 100|''Billboard'' Hot 100]]. It also reached No. 4 in Canada and was a No. 1 hit on both the US and Canadian Adult Contemporary charts. The song's accompanying music video received [[heavy rotation]] on [[MTV Europe]].<ref>{{cite magazine|first=|last=|title=Station Reports > TV > MTV/London|url=https://worldradiohistory.com/UK/Music-and-Media/90s/1991/MM-1991-02-23.pdf|magazine=[[Music & Media]]|date=23 February 1991|page=29|accessdate=12 December 2022}}</ref> "Cry for Help" was awarded one of [[Broadcast Music, Inc.|BMI]]'s Pop Awards in 1993, honoring the songwriters, composers and music publishers of the song.<ref>{{cite magazine |title= BMI Congratulates The PRS Writers and Publishers of BMI's Most Performed Works of 1992: Million Performance Songs |magazine= [[Music Week]] |page= 2 |date= 20 November 1993 |accessdate= 13 May 2023 |url= https://worldradiohistory.com/UK/Music-Week/1993/Music-Week-1993-11-20.pdf}}</ref>

The other two singles from ''Free'' were not as successful; "[[Move Right Out]]" reached No. 58 in the UK and No. 81 in the US, and "[[Never Knew Love (Rick Astley song)|Never Knew Love]]" reached No. 70 in the UK and did not chart in the US. ''Free'' marked the end of Astley's successful four-year period, and "Cry for Help" was the last Astley single to make the Top 10 in either the UK or US. Astley's next album, ''[[Body & Soul (Rick Astley album)|Body and Soul]]'', was released in 1993 and was largely an [[Adult Contemporary]] album. By the time the album was released, Astley had decided to retire from the music industry. As a result, the album did not get much promotion;<ref name="RickBio"/> it did not chart in the UK but managed to make the [[Billboard 200|''Billboard'' 200]], peaking at No. 182.

The two singles, "[[The Ones You Love]]" and "[[Hopelessly]]", performed very well on the US Adult Contemporary chart, peaking at No. 19 and No. 4 respectively. "The Ones You Love" peaked at No. 48 in the UK but did not chart in the US. "Hopelessly" crossed over and peaked at No. 28 on the US [[Billboard Hot 100|''Billboard'' Hot 100]], staying in the US Top 40 for five weeks, and No. 33 in the UK. It was the last hit to chart on the [[Top 40]] in either the UK or US. It was named as one of the most performed songs at the 1994 [[Broadcast Music Incorporated|BMI Awards]],<ref>{{cite web |url=http://eightiesclub.tripod.com/id229.htm |title=The Eighties Club |website=Eightiesclub.tripod.com |access-date=11 June 2013 |archive-date=14 July 2012 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120714095109/http://eightiesclub.tripod.com/id229.htm |url-status=dead }}</ref> and is one of the few songs to achieve BMI "Million-Air" status.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://chartts.tripod.com/AIR/usair1gl.htm |title=BMI Million-Airs |website=Chartts.tripod.com |access-date=11 June 2013 |archive-date=7 July 2012 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120707064125/http://chartts.tripod.com/AIR/usair1gl.htm |url-status=live }}</ref>

===1994–2000: Retirement===
Astley retired from the music industry in 1993 at the age of 27, deciding that family life was more important to him; "Now it doesn't seem like any amount of years, but then it was a quarter of my life. I was a young guy and I was like, 'I don't want to be doing that every single day of my life. I want to hang out with my friends. I've made a lot of money. I want to spend some of it. I want to do the things I want to do."<ref name="tone"/>

During his time out of the music business, his daughter Emilie (born in 1992) grew up.<ref>{{cite news|title=Greta singt seinen Song - Rick Astley (55) findet es "fantastisch"|trans-title=Greta sings his song - Rick Astley (55) finds it "fantastic"|language=de|url=https://de.euronews.com/2021/10/18/greta-singt-seinen-song-rick-astley-55-findet-es-fantastisch|publisher=[[Euronews]]|date=18 October 2021|access-date=5 January 2023|quote=1993 hatte sich Rick Astley im Alter von nur 27 Jahren aus der Musikbranche zurückgezogen, um sich ganz dem Familienleben zu widmen und seine 1992 geborene Tochter... aufzuziehen.|trans-quote=In 1993 at the age of just 27, Rick Astley withdrew himself from the music industry to devote his time entirely to family life and raising his daughter... born in 1992.|archive-date=5 January 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230105143728/https://de.euronews.com/2021/10/18/greta-singt-seinen-song-rick-astley-55-findet-es-fantastisch|url-status=live}}</ref> For the rest of the 1990s and early-2000s, Astley remained out of the public eye. He said later this was due to his growing frustration with the business side of things; he compared his life as a [[pop star]] to being "more like a travelling salesman than a musician or performer".<ref name=making/><ref name="surprisecomeback">{{cite web|last1=Agius|first1=Nicola|title=Eighties legend Rick Astley, 50, wows fans – including Amanda Holden – with shock comeback single|url=https://www.mirror.co.uk/tv/tv-news/eighties-legend-rick-astley-50-7705932|website=Daily Mirror|access-date=8 April 2016|date=7 April 2016|archive-date=7 April 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160407162846/http://www.mirror.co.uk/tv/tv-news/eighties-legend-rick-astley-50-7705932|url-status=live}}</ref> During that period he co-wrote "Mission Statement", a track for former [[Marillion]] singer [[Fish (singer)|Fish]]'s 1999 solo album ''[[Raingods with Zippos]]''.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://fish-thecompany.com/int/int34.htm | title=Interview 14th May 1999 | work=Fish-TheCompany.Com: Official Site | first=Ian | last=Oakley | date=14 May 1999 | access-date=22 June 2015 | archive-date=31 January 2016 | archive-url=https://archive.today/20160131134552/http://fish-thecompany.com/int/int34.htm | url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url=http://myglobalmind.com/2014/09/12/interview-fish-vocals-fish-former-marillion/ | title=Interview with Fish (Vocals) (Fish, Former Marillion) | work=Myglobalmind Webzine | first=Duncan | last=Everson | date=12 September 2014 | access-date=22 June 2015 | archive-date=31 January 2016 | archive-url=https://archive.today/20160131134557/http://myglobalmind.com/2014/09/12/interview-fish-vocals-fish-former-marillion/ | url-status=live }}</ref>

===2000–2020: Return to singing and renewed success===
Seven years after ''Body and Soul'', Astley returned to the music industry, signing a co-publishing deal with [[Polydor Records|Polydor]] and recording a new album, ''[[Keep It Turned On]]'', between November 2000 and September 2001, and released in late 2001. The album featured the single "[[Sleeping (Rick Astley song)|Sleeping]]", which became a minor club hit, thanks to a set of remixes from the US [[house music|house]] producer [[Todd Terry]]. ''Keep It Turned On'' was only released in [[Continental Europe]].<ref name="RickBio"/> Astley's first compilation album, ''Greatest Hits'', was released in 2002, and reached No. 16 on the [[UK Albums Chart]]. With no promotion from him, it sold over 100,000 copies and was certified Gold by the [[British Phonographic Industry]].<ref name="RickBio"/> In 2003, Astley charted at No. 10 in the UK as a songwriter with "[[Shakespeare's (Way With) Words]]" performed by short-lived boy band [[One True Voice]]. In 2004, Astley toured for the first time in fourteen years, which led him to a record contract with [[Sony BMG]].<ref name="RickBio"/>

In March 2005, Astley released the album ''[[Portrait (Rick Astley album)|Portrait]]'', in which he [[cover version|covered]] many classic standards such as "[[Vincent (Don McLean song)|Vincent]]",<ref>{{cite web|format=video|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ttfTJuU3frA|last=Astley|first=Rick|title=Vincent (Live)| date=17 July 2007 |via=YouTube|access-date=22 December 2015|archive-date=8 February 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140208004513/http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ttfTJuU3frA&app=desktop|url-status=live}}</ref> "[[Nature Boy (song)|Nature Boy]]" and "[[(They Long to Be) Close to You|Close to You]]". Astley and Sony BMG were unhappy with the result, so the album was poorly promoted,<ref name="RickBio"/> yet it managed to reach No. 26 on the [[UK Albums Chart]]. In April 2008, the album ''The Ultimate Collection: Rick Astley'' was released by Sony BMG, and by May it had reached No. 17 on the UK Top 40 Albums Chart,<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/entertainment/7383141.stm|work=BBC News|title=Madonna celebrates chart double|date=4 May 2008|access-date=22 May 2010|archive-date=7 May 2008|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080507032234/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/entertainment/7383141.stm|url-status=live}}</ref> again with no promotion by Astley.

[[File:Rick Astley Tivoli Gardens.jpg|thumb|Astley performing in Copenhagen, Denmark, July 2009|256x256px]]

In September 2008, Astley was nominated for the "Best Act Ever" award at the [[MTV Europe Music Awards]].<ref name="bbc1" /> The push to make him the winner of the award continued after the announcement, as did efforts to encourage MTV to invite Astley to the awards ceremony.<ref>[http://www.bestactever.com/2008/10/10/wtf-mtv/ Bestactever.com 10 October 2008] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081014103132/http://www.bestactever.com/2008/10/10/wtf-mtv/ |date=14 October 2008 }}. Retrieved 12 October 2008.</ref> On 7 November, following a massive Internet campaign by fans, Astley won the award in Liverpool but was not there in person to receive it. [[Perez Hilton]] collected the prize on his behalf.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/entertainment/7714498.stm|title=US stars scoop MTV Europe awards|work=BBC News|date=7 November 2008|access-date=22 November 2008|archive-date=11 July 2012|archive-url=https://archive.today/20120711065539/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/entertainment/7714498.stm|url-status=live}}</ref> On the back of this, "Never Gonna Give You Up" (which had recently become an [[Internet meme]] via [[Rickrolling]]) returned to the UK charts, peaking at No. 73 during the Christmas period. Astley continued touring across the globe and, in 2008, performed in the ''[[Here and Now Tour (various artists)|Here and Now Tour]]'' which featured various other 1980s acts such as [[Boy George]] and [[Belinda Carlisle]].<ref name="RickBio"/> In April 2009, he wrote an article for ''[[Time Magazine|Time]]'' about [[moot (4chan)|moot]].<ref>{{cite magazine|url=http://www.time.com/time/specials/packages/article/0,28804,1894410_1893837_1894180,00.html|magazine=Time|title=The 2009 Time 100|date=30 April 2009|access-date=22 May 2010|archive-date=5 January 2013|archive-url=https://archive.today/20130105053232/http://www.time.com/time/specials/packages/article/0,28804,1894410_1893837_1894180,00.html|url-status=dead}}</ref>

Astley was a special guest throughout [[Peter Kay]]'s new tour, ''[[The Tour That Doesn't Tour Tour...Now On Tour]]'', from 27 April to 22 May 2010. To mark the occasion, Astley released a new single, "[[Lights Out (Rick Astley song)|Lights Out]]", on his own label on 7 June 2010.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.famemagazine.co.uk/2010/04/28/rick-astley-returns-with-peter-kay/|title=Rick Astley Returns With Peter Kay|work=Fame Magazine|date=28 April 2010|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160221012057/http://www.famemagazine.co.uk/2010/04/28/rick-astley-returns-with-peter-kay/|archive-date=21 February 2016}}</ref> It was his first release in the [[UK Singles Chart]] in seventeen years. It was well received by radio, peaking at No. 15 on the UK Airplay Charts, but failed to become a commercial hit, reaching only No. 97 on the [[UK Singles Chart]].

[[File:Rick Astley performing at Let's Rock Bristol, 2014.jpg|thumb|upright|left|Astley performing at [[Bristol]], June 2014]]

During the summer of 2010, Astley became a radio DJ for London's [[Magic Radio (London radio station)|Magic FM]], presenting a Sunday show.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.magic.co.uk/sectional.asp?id=38003|title=Rick Astley's Summer Soundtrack|publisher=Magic FM 105.7}}{{dead link|date=December 2012}}</ref><ref>{{cite news| url=https://www.theguardian.com/media/2010/jul/05/rick-astley-magic-radio| title=Rick Astley to host Magic 105.4 Sunday show| newspaper=The Guardian| location=London| first=Josh| last=Halliday| date=5 July 2010| access-date=16 December 2016| archive-date=6 March 2016| archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160306162448/http://www.theguardian.com/media/2010/jul/05/rick-astley-magic-radio| url-status=live}}</ref> The initial contract was for eight weeks, but he proved popular with listeners and his contract was extended till the end of the year.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.bauermedia.co.uk/Press-Office/News/Magic-1054-extends-Rick-Astleys-Show/ |title=Magic 105.4 Extends Rick Astley's Show |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120309050454/http://www.bauermedia.co.uk/Press-Office/News/Magic-1054-extends-Rick-Astleys-Show/ |archive-date=9 March 2012 }}</ref> In December 2010, Astley co-hosted the ''[[Chris Evans Breakfast Show]]'' on [[BBC Radio 2]] with Peter Kay,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/programmes/b00wdf15|title=Friday – It's Kay Day!|publisher=BBC|access-date=14 December 2010|archive-date=1 August 2012|archive-url=https://archive.today/20120801092722/http://www.bbc.co.uk/programmes/b00wdf15|url-status=live}}</ref> and in March 2011 appeared in [[Comic Relief]]'s [[Red Nose Day 2011|Red Nose Day]] telethon on the [[BBC]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Mn6_60oLrfI|title=The Chefs Choir Sing 'Never Gonna Give You Up' – Red Nose Day 2011|date=18 March 2011 |via=YouTube|access-date=29 March 2011|archive-date=22 March 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110322044404/http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Mn6_60oLrfI|url-status=live}}</ref>

In April 2016, Astley released "[[Keep Singing]]", from his forthcoming album ''[[50 (Rick Astley album)|50]]'' which was released on 10 June 2016 by [[BMG Rights Management|BMG]]. Interviewed by [[Amanda Holden]] on the ''[[Lorraine (TV programme)|Lorraine]]'' show on 7 April, Astley stated that turning 50 had prompted him to release the single. He said, "It was a big milestone. I got back in the studio and friends were telling me the material I was working on was pretty good. So I decided to go for it."<ref>{{cite web |url=http://metro.co.uk/2016/04/07/80s-legend-rick-astley-has-just-released-a-comeback-single-23-years-after-his-retirement-5801745 |title=Rick Astley releases comeback single Keep Singing 23 years after 'retirement' |website=Metro |location=UK |date=7 April 2016 |access-date=13 April 2016 |archive-date=11 April 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160411145044/http://metro.co.uk/2016/04/07/80s-legend-rick-astley-has-just-released-a-comeback-single-23-years-after-his-retirement-5801745/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="surprisecomeback"/><ref>{{cite news|last1=Golgowsk|first1=Nina|title=Rick Astley Releases New Single And We Swear It's Not A Rickroll|url=http://www.huffingtonpost.com/entry/rick-astley-releases-single_us_5706615ce4b053766188cb89|newspaper=HuffPost|access-date=8 April 2016|date=7 April 2016|archive-date=7 April 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160407211014/http://www.huffingtonpost.com/entry/rick-astley-releases-single_us_5706615ce4b053766188cb89|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last1=Fitzpatrick |first1=Katie |title=Rick Astley will perform and sign copies of his new album at HMV Manchester |url=http://www.manchestereveningnews.co.uk/news/showbiz-news/rick-astley-hmv-manchester-signing--11409007 |access-date=2 June 2016 |work=men |date=31 May 2016 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160602042810/http://www.manchestereveningnews.co.uk/news/showbiz-news/rick-astley-hmv-manchester-signing--11409007 |archive-date=2 June 2016 }}</ref> The album reached No. 1 on the Official UK Album Sales charts in the week of 17 June to 23 June 2016. On 30 June 2017, Astley announced on his Twitter account that ''50'' had been certified Platinum in the United Kingdom (300,000 and more albums sold).<ref name=50no1>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/entertainment-arts-36561862|title=Resurgent Rick Astley tops UK album chart|publisher=BBC|date=17 June 2016|access-date=20 June 2016|last1=Savage|first1=Mark|archive-date=19 June 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160619235022/http://www.bbc.com/news/entertainment-arts-36561862|url-status=live}}</ref>

In September 2016, Astley released the single "Dance", together with an official video.<ref>{{youTube|5o7WEv5Q5ZE|Rick Astley – Dance (Official Video) }}</ref> On 1 June 2018, Astley posted a video on [[Twitter]] announcing his upcoming album ''[[Beautiful Life (Rick Astley album)|Beautiful Life]]'' which was released on 13 July.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://twitter.com/rickastley/status/1002505537404243969|title=Rick Astley on Twitter|website=Twitter|access-date=9 June 2018|archive-date=17 October 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211017100501/https://twitter.com/rickastley/status/1002505537404243969|url-status=live}}</ref> Astley did a tour, the ''#BeautifulLife Tour'', and tickets were first available for purchase to anyone who pre-ordered the album.<ref name="JourneyStockPodcast-27" /> ''Beautiful Life'' peaked at No. 6 on the UK Albums Chart. In 2019, Astley served as the support act on [[Take That]]'s [[Greatest Hits Live (Take That)|Greatest Hits Live]] 38-date tour in Britain and Ireland.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.officialcharts.com/chart-news/take-thats-greatest-hits-live-2019-tour-dates-will-be-supported-by-rick-astley__26152/|title=Take That's tour support has been announced|website=Officialcharts.com|access-date=3 June 2019|archive-date=30 April 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190430033939/https://www.officialcharts.com/chart-news/take-thats-greatest-hits-live-2019-tour-dates-will-be-supported-by-rick-astley__26152/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Kirwin-Jones|first=Ellie|date=11 October 2020|title=Rick Astley talks 'massive egos' after supporting Take That on tour: 'We can all pretend'|url=https://www.express.co.uk/celebrity-news/1344796/Rick-Astley-Take-That-tour-egos-fans-Never-Gonna-Give-You-Up-news-latest-update|access-date=7 September 2021|website=Express.co.uk|language=en|archive-date=7 September 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210907002927/https://www.express.co.uk/celebrity-news/1344796/Rick-Astley-Take-That-tour-egos-fans-Never-Gonna-Give-You-Up-news-latest-update|url-status=live}}</ref> He is also an occasional guest vocalist at [[Foo Fighters]] concerts,<ref name="savage">{{cite news|last=Savage|first=Mark|date=24 October 2019|title=Rick Astley: Inside the home studio where he masterminded his comeback|work=[[BBC News]]|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/entertainment-arts-50100112|access-date=25 October 2019|archive-date=24 October 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191024151045/https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/entertainment-arts-50100112|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|first=Mike|last=Julianelle|date=26 August 2019|title=Dave Grohl Rickrolls 60,000 UK Fans with Surprise Rick Astley Duet|url=https://www.thedad.com/dave-grohl-rickrolls-london-fans-with-surprise-rick-astley-duet/|access-date=7 September 2021|website=The Dad|language=en-US|archive-date=7 September 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210907105744/https://www.thedad.com/dave-grohl-rickrolls-london-fans-with-surprise-rick-astley-duet/|url-status=live}}</ref> and has previously performed with [[A-ha]] and [[Kylie Minogue]].<ref>{{Cite web|last1=Wehner |first1=Cyclone |title=a-ha, Rick Astley @ Margaret Court Arena|url=https://themusic.com.au/reviews/a-ha-margaret-court-arena-cyclone/OYchLSwvLlE/24-02-20/|access-date=7 September 2021|website=The Music|language=en|archive-date=7 September 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210907002928/https://themusic.com.au/reviews/a-ha-margaret-court-arena-cyclone/OYchLSwvLlE/24-02-20/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|date=11 September 2018|title=Watch Kylie Minogue Duet With Rick Astley On 'Never Gonna Give You Up'|url=https://musicfeeds.com.au/news/watch-kylie-minogue-duet-with-rick-astley-on-never-gonna-give-you-up/|access-date=7 September 2021|website=Music Feeds|archive-date=7 September 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210907005931/https://musicfeeds.com.au/news/watch-kylie-minogue-duet-with-rick-astley-on-never-gonna-give-you-up/|url-status=live}}</ref>

Astley and the [[Foo Fighters]] both performed at the [[Summer Sonic Festival]] in Japan. The band invited Astley to join them as they performed "Never Gonna Give You Up" in the style of "[[Smells Like Teen Spirit]]". Astley and Foo Fighters leader [[Dave Grohl]] became friends and he performed with the band at the [[California Jam]] rock festival.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/entertainment/books/2025/01/21/rick-astley-memoir-highlights/77676712007/|title=Rick Astley tackles Rickrolling, Dave Grohl and resurgent pop career in 'Never' memoir|last=Ruggieri|first=Melissa|work=[[USA Today]]|date=January 21, 2025|access-date=February 2, 2025}}</ref>

During live performances where he sings "Never Gonna Give You Up", Astley sometimes shouts out to the audience that he is feeling "bloody marvellous" or that he is feeling "fantastic" to have some banter with the audience.<ref>{{Cite news|last=Whitehead|first=Mat|date=21 August 2017|title=The Foo Fighters And Rick Astley Just Trolled A Whole Festival Crowd With The Perfect Rickroll|language=en-AU|work=Huffington Post|url=https://www.huffingtonpost.com.au/2017/08/21/the-foo-fighters-and-rick-astley-just-trolled-a-whole-festival-crowd-with-the-perfect-rickroll_a_23155255/|access-date=7 September 2021|archive-date=7 September 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210907074716/https://www.huffingtonpost.com.au/2017/08/21/the-foo-fighters-and-rick-astley-just-trolled-a-whole-festival-crowd-with-the-perfect-rickroll_a_23155255/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Johnson|first=Helen|date=20 August 2017|title=Rick Astley did a sweary 'Never Gonna Give You Up' with the Foo Fighters|url=http://www.manchestereveningnews.co.uk/whats-on/whats-on-news/rick-astley-sweary-never-gonna-13504620|access-date=7 September 2021|website=Manchester Evening News|language=en|archive-date=7 September 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210907075755/https://www.manchestereveningnews.co.uk/whats-on/whats-on-news/rick-astley-sweary-never-gonna-13504620|url-status=live}}</ref> On 12 October 2019, Astley appeared on ''[[CBeebies Bedtime Stories]]''.<ref>{{Cite web|title=CBeebies Bedtime Stories Catch up CBEEBIES. Watch all episodes from "CBeebies Bedtime Stories" on demand. Catchupplayer.co.uk|url=https://www.catchupplayer.co.uk/episode/137657/Cbeebies_Bedtime_Stories.html|access-date=2021-09-15|website=Catchupplayer.co.uk|language=en|archive-date=15 September 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210915102723/https://www.catchupplayer.co.uk/episode/137657/Cbeebies_Bedtime_Stories.html|url-status=live}}</ref> On 25 October, he released a [[greatest hits album]], ''[[The Best of Me (Rick Astley album)|The Best of Me]]''. The album includes the single "[[Every One of Us (song)|Every One of Us]]", which was released in September 2019. ''The Best of Me'' reached No. 4 on the [[UK Albums Chart]] and No. 1 on the [[UK Independent Singles and Albums Charts|UK Independent Albums]] chart.<ref>{{cite web|via=[[YouTube]]|title=Rick Astley – Never Gonna Give You Up (Pianoforte) (Official Audio)|date=21 October 2019 |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GHMjD0Lp5DY|access-date=25 October 2019|archive-date=26 November 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191126214410/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GHMjD0Lp5DY|url-status=live}}</ref>

=== 2020–present: Covers and ''Are We There Yet?'' ===
In June 2020, towards the end of [[COVID-19 lockdowns|the nationwide lockdown]] in England due to the [[COVID-19 pandemic]], Astley released four cover songs: "[[Ain't No Sunshine]]" by [[Bill Withers]], "[[Everlong]]" by Foo Fighters, "[[Titanium (David Guetta song)|Titanium]]" by [[David Guetta]], and "[[Better Now]]" by [[Post Malone]]. Around that time, Astley made a [[TikTok]] account, launching it with a video of him dancing to "Never Gonna Give You Up" which received millions of views.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Rick Astley rickrolls TikTok, scores millions of views|url=https://www.owensbororadio.com/2020/11/05/rick-astley-rickrolls-tiktok-scores-millions-of-views/|date=5 November 2020|access-date=7 September 2021|website=Owensboro Radio|language=en|archive-date=21 May 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240521125425/https://owensbororadio.com/2020/11/05/rick-astley-rickrolls-tiktok-scores-millions-of-views/|url-status=live}}</ref> On 30 November 2020, Astley released a single, "Love This Christmas", the video featuring Astley performing amid a flurry of [[snow]] and dancing [[Snowman|snowmen]]. To accompany the single, he launched a signed limited edition [[Christmas]] card, with all proceeds going to the [[Shooting Star Children's Hospices|Shooting Stars Children's Hospices]].<ref>{{Cite web|date=11 December 2020|title=Rick Astley returns with new single Love This Christmas|url=https://www.classicpopmag.com/2020/12/rick-astley-returns-with-new-single-love-this-christmas/|access-date=1 January 2021|website=Classic Pop Magazine|language=en-US|archive-date=17 January 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210117051348/https://www.classicpopmag.com/2020/12/rick-astley-returns-with-new-single-love-this-christmas/|url-status=live}}</ref> On 7 April 2021, Astley released a single, "Unwanted", inspired by the era and vibes of the original podcast.<ref>{{cite web|via=[[YouTube]]|title=Rick Astley - Unwanted (Official Song from the Podcast) (Lyric Video)|date=8 April 2021 |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lU1EmTA4J60|access-date=8 April 2021|archive-date=8 April 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210408170636/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lU1EmTA4J60|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 2 September 2021, amid the news that [[ABBA]] had reunited, Astley released a cover of their song "[[The Winner Takes It All]]".<ref>{{Cite web|title=Rick Astley's stunning cover of ABBA's 'The Winner Takes it All' will give you goosebumps - video|url=https://www.smoothradio.com/artists/abba/rick-astley-winner-takes-it-all-cover-video/|access-date=4 September 2021|website=Smoothradio.com|language=en|archive-date=4 September 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210904072953/https://www.smoothradio.com/artists/abba/rick-astley-winner-takes-it-all-cover-video/|url-status=live}}</ref> That month, he and the band [[Blossoms (band)|Blossoms]] performed two concerts of covers by [[the Smiths]], who had disbanded in the 1980s.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Lavin |first=Will |date=2021-10-09 |title=See footage from first night of Blossoms and Rick Astley's Smiths cover shows |url=https://www.nme.com/news/music/see-footage-from-first-night-of-blossoms-and-rick-astleys-smiths-cover-shows-3066376 |access-date=2023-06-25 |website=[[NME]] |language=en-GB |archive-date=21 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240521125425/https://www.nme.com/news/music/see-footage-from-first-night-of-blossoms-and-rick-astleys-smiths-cover-shows-3066376 |url-status=live }}</ref> ''[[The Guardian]]'' gave one performance four out of five, writing that "the ultimate Smiths karaoke shouldn't work, but it does".<ref name="Simpson-2021" /> Astley and Blossoms performed again at the [[Glastonbury Festival 2023|2023 Glastonbury Festival]]. ''The Guardian'' gave the performances positive reviews, suggesting that they offered fans a way to enjoy the Smiths without the "moral queasiness" of the Smiths singer [[Morrissey]], who had become a controversial figure in the preceding years.<ref name="Simpson-2021">{{Cite news |last=Simpson |first=Dave |date=2021-10-10 |title=Rick Astley and Blossoms review – the ultimate Smiths karaoke shouldn't work, but it does |language=en-GB |work=[[The Guardian]] |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2021/oct/10/rick-astley-blossoms-review-smiths-karaoke-albert-hall-manchester |access-date=2023-06-25 |issn=0261-3077 |archive-date=21 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240521125535/https://www.theguardian.com/music/2021/oct/10/rick-astley-blossoms-review-smiths-karaoke-albert-hall-manchester |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Williams |first=Jenessa |date=2023-06-24 |title=Blossoms and Rick Astley at Glastonbury review – Smiths hits are the very opposite of miserable now |language=en-GB |work=[[The Guardian]] |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2023/jun/24/blossoms-rick-astley-glastonbury-review-smiths-covers-morrissey |access-date=2023-06-25 |issn=0261-3077 |archive-date=21 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240521125553/https://www.theguardian.com/music/2023/jun/24/blossoms-rick-astley-glastonbury-review-smiths-covers-morrissey |url-status=live }}</ref> Astley said he performed "from a place of absolute joy and love for those songs".<ref name="Trendell-2023">{{Cite web |last=Trendell |first=Andrew |date=2023-06-24 |title=Rick Astley tells us about his Glastonbury secret set of Smiths covers with Blossoms |url=https://www.nme.com/news/music/rick-astley-blossoms-glastonbury-secret-set-interview-morrissey-johnny-marr-3459852 |access-date=2023-06-25 |website=[[NME]] |language=en-GB |archive-date=30 June 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230630074245/https://www.nme.com/news/music/rick-astley-blossoms-glastonbury-secret-set-interview-morrissey-johnny-marr-3459852 |url-status=live }}</ref> He also played a solo set at Glastonbury that year, his first appearance there.<ref>{{cite web |last=Savage |first=Mark |date=24 June 2023 |title=Rick Astley doesn't let Glastonbury down |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/entertainment-arts-66007741 |access-date=24 June 2023 |work=BBC News |publisher=BBC |archive-date=24 June 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230624121814/https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/entertainment-arts-66007741 |url-status=live }}</ref>

Morrissey thanked Blossoms and Astley on his website, writing: "Anything that generates interest in that tired old Smiths warhorse is testimony to the wallop it packed."<ref name="Trendell-2023" /> [[Johnny Marr]] described the performances as "funny and horrible at the same time" and said he had not been informed they were happening. He said later he had "dealt with it".<ref>{{Cite web |last=Trendell |first=Andrew |date=2021-09-24 |title=Johnny Marr puts Blossoms and Rick Astley Smiths covers row to bed: "I've dealt with it" |url=https://www.nme.com/news/music/johnny-marr-puts-blossoms-and-rick-astley-smiths-covers-row-to-bed-ive-dealt-with-it-3054333 |access-date=2023-06-25 |website=[[NME]] |language=en-GB}}</ref> In response to Marr, Astley said: "It's his work and his life, but it doesn't seem like [the Smiths] are ever getting back together ... It's a difficult one, but I did it from a place of absolute joy and love for those songs."<ref name="Trendell-2023" />

In October 2023, Astley released ''[[Are We There Yet? (Rick Astley album)|Are We There Yet?]]'', his first album of new music since 2018's ''Beautiful Life''. Inspired by [[soul music]],<ref name="AS">{{cite magazine|url=https://americansongwriter.com/rick-astley-reveals-upcoming-album-are-we-there-yet/|title=Rick Astley Reveals Upcoming Album ''Are We There Yet?''|magazine=[[American Songwriter]]|last=Benitez-Eves|first=Tina|date=20 June 2023|access-date=17 October 2023|archive-date=21 September 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230921044817/https://americansongwriter.com/rick-astley-reveals-upcoming-album-are-we-there-yet/|url-status=live}}</ref> it debuted at No. 2 on the [[UK Albums Chart]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://news.sky.com/story/who-is-ren-welsh-singer-songwriter-pips-rick-astley-to-uk-number-one-album-12987716|title=Who is Ren? Welsh singer-songwriter pips Rick Astley to UK number one album|website=[[Sky News]]|last=Evans|first=Tomos|date=20 October 2023|access-date=22 October 2023|archive-date=22 October 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231022025056/https://news.sky.com/story/who-is-ren-welsh-singer-songwriter-pips-rick-astley-to-uk-number-one-album-12987716|url-status=live}}</ref> Astley's autobiography, ''Never: The Autobiography'', was released on 10 October 2024.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Gotto |first=Connor |date=2024-02-12 |title=Rick Astley sets October release for new memoir Never |url=https://retropopmagazine.com/rick-astley-sets-october-release-for-new-memoir-never/ |access-date=2024-07-10 |website=RETROPOP |language=en-GB}}</ref> The book received positive reviews from critics with John Aizlewood from ''[[i (British newspaper)|i]]'' calling it a "nuanced memoir" and a "revelation".<ref>{{Cite web |last=Aizlewood|first=John|date=October 5, 2024 |title=Never by Rick Astley: You wouldn't get this from any other guy|url=https://inews.co.uk/culture/books/never-by-rick-astley-you-wouldnt-get-this-from-any-other-guy-3302146 |website=inews.co.uk|language=en-GB}}</ref>

In March 2025, Rick Astley uploaded a cover of [[Chappell Roan]]'s popular song "[[Pink Pony Club]]" to his YouTube channel.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Aniftos |first=Rania |date=2025-03-04 |title=Watch Rick Astley's Acoustic Cover of Chappell Roan's 'Pink Pony Club' |url=https://www.billboard.com/music/pop/rick-astley-chappell-roan-pink-pony-club-cover-1235914966/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250304224434/https://www.billboard.com/music/pop/rick-astley-chappell-roan-pink-pony-club-cover-1235914966/ |archive-date=2025-03-04 |access-date=2025-08-12 |magazine=Billboard |language=en-US}}</ref>

In July 2025, Astley was honored at the [[Silver Clef Award|Silver Clef Awards]] being given the Outstanding Achievement Award.<ref>{{Cite web |title=O2 Silver Clefs honour The Last Dinner Party, David Gilmour, Noah Kahan, Aurora, Soul II Soul & more |url=https://www.musicweek.com/talent/read/o2-silver-clefs-honour-the-last-dinner-party-david-gilmour-noah-kahan-aurora-soul-ii-soul-more/092235 |access-date=2025-08-30 |website=www.musicweek.com |language=en}}</ref>

On 6 February 2026, his 60th birthday, Astley released the single "Waiting on You".<ref>{{Cite magazine |url=https://www.classicpopmag.com/news/rick-astley-celebrates-60th-birthday-with-new-single-waiting-on-you/ |title=Rick Astley celebrates 60th birthday with new single Waiting On You |last=Biggane |first=Dan |date=6 February 2026 |access-date=11 March 2026 |magazine=Classic Pop}}</ref> This was followed by "Raindrops", a single released in April 2026.<ref>{{Cite magazine |url=https://www.classicpopmag.com/news/rick-astley-shares-his-new-single-raindrops/ |title=Rick Astley shares his new single Raindrops |last=Biggane |first=Dan |date=10 April 2026 |access-date=10 April 2026 |magazine=Classic Pop}}</ref>

Astley started the Reflection Tour on 10 April 2026 at the [[OVO Hydro]] in Glasgow.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.scotsman.com/whats-on/rick-astley-glasgow-tour-tickets-presale-prices-support-5296300 |title=Rick Astley to play huge show in Glasgow - here's how to get tickets, presale and everything you need to know |last=Jack |first=Lauren |date=1 September 2025 |access-date=10 April 2026 |work=The Scotsman}}</ref><ref>{{Cite magazine |url=https://www.nme.com/news/music/rick-astley-announces-reflection-2026-uk-and-ireland-tour-dates-3889062 |title=Rick Astley announces 'Reflection' 2026 UK and Ireland tour dates |last=Dunworth |first=Liberty |date=1 September 2025 |access-date=10 April 2026 |magazine=NME}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.heraldscotland.com/life_style/26013361.astley-dives-80s-fans-relive-youth-city-gig/ |title=Review: Rick Astley, Glasgow Hydro **** |last=Jamieson |first=Teddy |date=11 April 2026 |access-date=14 April 2026 |work=The Herald |location=Glasgow}}</ref>

==Legacy==
{{Main|Rickrolling}}
[[File:Rick Astley Macy Parade cropped.jpg|thumb|Astley rickrolling the 2008 [[Macy's Thanksgiving Day Parade]]]]
In 2007,<!--EDITORS PLEASE READ. THE RICKROLL INTERNET MEME BEGAN IN 2007, PLEASE DO NOT CHANGE THE DATE TO 2008 BECAUSE THIS IS NOT ACCURATE.--><ref name="Date">{{cite web|url=http://www.google.com/trends?q=rick+astley%2C+rickroll%2C+rick+roll%2C+never+gonna+give+you+up&ctab=0&geo=all&date=2007&sort=0|title=Rick Roll related Google Trends|publisher=[[Google Trends]]|access-date=4 April 2008|archive-date=21 May 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240521125539/https://trends.google.com/trends?q=rick+astley%2C+rickroll%2C+rick+roll%2C+never+gonna+give+you+up&ctab=0&geo=all&date=2007&sort=0|url-status=live}}</ref> Astley became the subject of a [[viral phenomenon|viral]] [[Internet meme]] known as [[rickrolling]]. The meme is a type of [[bait and switch]] using a disguised [[hyperlink]] that leads to the "Never Gonna Give You Up" music video. When victims click on a seemingly unrelated link, the site with the music video loads instead of what was expected and in doing so they are said to have been "rickrolled". The meme has also extended to using the song's lyrics in unexpected places. The meme gained mainstream attention in 2008 through several publicized events, particularly when [[YouTube]] used it on its 2008 [[April Fools' Day]] event.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://techcrunch.com/2008/03/31/youtube-rickrolls-users/|title=YouTube RickRolls Users|date=April 2008 |publisher=[[TechCrunch]]|access-date=1 June 2008|archive-date=29 August 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120829200506/http://techcrunch.com/2008/03/31/youtube-rickrolls-users/|url-status=live}}</ref> Rickrolling saw a massive resurgence online in the early 2020s. In online classes on [[Zoom (software)|Zoom]] during the worldwide [[COVID-19 lockdowns|COVID-19 lockdown]], students often rickrolled their classmates and teachers.<ref>{{Cite web|title=You Can Now 'Rick Roll' Your Zoom Meetings|url=https://nerdist.com/article/you-can-now-rick-roll-your-zoom-meetings/|access-date=4 March 2021|website=Nerdist|date=7 July 2020 |archive-date=9 October 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211009133400/https://nerdist.com/article/you-can-now-rick-roll-your-zoom-meetings/|url-status=live}}</ref> A 4K remaster of the "Never Gonna Give You Up" music video went viral in early 2021.<ref>{{Cite web|date=18 February 2021|title=Rick Astley's Rick Roll meme goes viral again with disturbing 4K remaster|url=https://www.dexerto.com/entertainment/rick-astleys-rick-roll-meme-goes-viral-again-with-disturbing-4k-remaster-1517065/|access-date=4 March 2021|website=Dexerto|language=en|archive-date=6 October 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211006013838/https://www.dexerto.com/entertainment/rick-astleys-rick-roll-meme-goes-viral-again-with-disturbing-4k-remaster-1517065/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|date=18 February 2021|title=Rickroll your eyeballs into oblivion with remastered "Never Gonna Give You Up": Watch|url=https://consequence.net/2021/02/rick-astley-never-gonna-give-you-up-remastered/|access-date=4 March 2021|website=Consequence of Sound|language=en-US|archive-date=18 May 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210518190014/https://consequence.net/2021/02/rick-astley-never-gonna-give-you-up-remastered/|url-status=live}}</ref>

Initially, Astley, who had only recently returned to performing after a 10-year hiatus, was hesitant about using his newfound popularity from the meme to further his career, but accepted the fame when he rickrolled the 2008 [[Macy's Thanksgiving Day Parade]] with a surprise performance of the song. Since then, he has seen his performance career revitalised by the meme's popularity. In an interview, Astley stated that one of his favourite rickroll videos is "BarackRoll", a rickroll featuring clips from rallies by [[Barack Obama]] during his [[Barack Obama 2008 presidential campaign|2008 presidential campaign]], edited to make it look as though he is singing "Never Gonna Give You Up".<ref>{{Cite web|title=Astley never stops appreciating 'Never Gonna Give You Up'|date=22 October 2016|url=https://www.taiwannews.com.tw/en/news/3004212|access-date=7 September 2021|website=Taiwan News|archive-date=7 September 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210907074715/https://www.taiwannews.com.tw/en/news/3004212|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{Cite magazine|title=Video Pick: Obama 'Sings' Rick Astley's Hit|language=en-US|magazine=Wired|url=https://www.wired.com/2008/08/obamas-never-go/|access-date=7 September 2021|issn=1059-1028|archive-date=21 May 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240521130932/https://www.wired.com/2008/08/obamas-never-go/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|date=20 October 2008|title=The Rickroll Gets Political|url=https://www.mentalfloss.com/article/19914/rickroll-gets-political|access-date=7 September 2021|website=Mentalfloss.com|language=en|archive-date=7 September 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210907074708/https://www.mentalfloss.com/article/19914/rickroll-gets-political|url-status=dead}}</ref>

Astley himself has been rickrolled a few times; the first time pre-dated the viral phenomenon. In an interview with [[Larry King]], Astley says that the first time he fell for the prank was through an email his friend sent him during the early 2000s.<ref>{{cite video|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=L0HVnaIRrFo |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/varchive/youtube/20211212/L0HVnaIRrFo| archive-date=2021-12-12 |url-status=live|title=Has Rick Astley ever been "Rick-rolled"?|date=25 January 2017|access-date=29 September 2020|work=[[Larry King Now]]|via=[[YouTube]]}}{{cbignore}}</ref> On a [[Reddit]] post in June 2020, a user claimed to have met Astley backstage when they were 12 years old, but the user posted a link to the song instead of a picture verifying the encounter. Astley later confirmed he had been tricked into clicking the link.<ref>{{Cite web|title=New Internet Legend Manages to Rick Roll Rick Astley|date=18 June 2020 |url=https://news.yahoo.com/internet-legend-manages-rick-roll-143025415.html|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200621105834/https://news.yahoo.com/internet-legend-manages-rick-roll-143025415.html|archive-date=21 June 2020|access-date=18 June 2020}}</ref><ref name="time_NewI2">{{Cite magazine|author=Melissa Locker|date=18 June 2020|title=New Internet Legend Manages to Rick Roll Rick Astley|url=https://time.com/5855001/rick-roll-rick-astley/|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200618192603/https://time.com/5855001/rick-roll-rick-astley/|archive-date=18 June 2020|access-date=19 June 2020|magazine=[[Time (magazine)|Time]]|quote=The trick was so seamlessly perfect that Astley had no choice but to applaud it by posting a clap emoji, and then called out the clever user in his sign off post, saying, "u/theMalleableDuck I salute you!"}}</ref> The post became the most-upvoted post of 2020 on Reddit.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Marcin|first1=Tim|date=8 December 2020|title=Rick Astley getting rickrolled was Reddit's most upvoted post in 2020|publisher=Mashable|url=https://mashable.com/article/reddit-rick-astley-rickrolled-top-post/|access-date=9 February 2021|archive-date=14 January 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220114200335/https://mashable.com/article/reddit-rick-astley-rickrolled-top-post|url-status=live}}</ref>

==Personal life==
Astley has been married to film producer Lene Bausager since 2013.<ref name="bbc">{{cite web |last1=Savage |first1=Mark |title=Rick Astley on his wife Lene: 'The success I'm having is due to her' |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/entertainment-arts-45082037 |website=BBC News |publisher=BBC |date=7 August 2018 |access-date=9 April 2021 |archive-date=7 November 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201107234326/https://www.bbc.com/news/entertainment-arts-45082037 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.thetimes.com/world/us-world/article/relative-values-the-1980s-pop-star-rick-astley-and-his-wife-lene-bausager-a-danish-film-producer-sx6xwj9kl|title=Relative Values: the 1980s pop star Rick Astley and his wife, Lene Bausager, a Danish film producer|first=Danny|last=Scott|date=15 July 2018|website=[[The Times]]|access-date=14 March 2026|url-access=limited|quote=Lene's my manager now and we did get married in 2013.}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://ew.com/article/2016/10/11/rick-astley/|title=Rick Astley: How the '80s star rolled back into the spotlight|first=Clark|last=Collis|date=11 October 2016|website=[[Entertainment Weekly]]|access-date=14 March 2026}}</ref> They met when she was working as a promoter with RCA in 1987,<ref name="Independent"/> and she is now a film producer.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.digitalhit.com/galleries/22/1/29|title=Photo 29 Lene Bausager and Rick Astley &#124; 78th Annual Academy Awards|website=Digitalhit.com|access-date=22 May 2010|archive-date=29 July 2012|archive-url=https://archive.today/20120729115917/http://www.digitalhit.com/galleries/22/1/29|url-status=live}}</ref> Their daughter has a master's degree in fine art and is a garden designer.<ref name="Rocca-2020">{{Cite web|last=Rocca|first=Jane|date=22 August 2020|title='I fell in love with her the day I met her': the women in Rick Astley's life|url=https://www.smh.com.au/culture/music/i-fell-in-love-with-her-the-day-i-met-her-the-women-in-rick-astley-s-life-20200819-p55n8s.html|access-date=4 September 2021|website=The Sydney Morning Herald|language=en|archive-date=4 September 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210904144120/https://www.smh.com.au/culture/music/i-fell-in-love-with-her-the-day-i-met-her-the-women-in-rick-astley-s-life-20200819-p55n8s.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Astley and his wife live in the [[Surrey]] town of [[Molesey]] and their daughter lives in [[Denmark]].<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.thetimes.com/life-style/property-home/article/rick-astley-interview-the-1980s-pop-star-on-diy-disasters-and-why-hes-never-gonna-get-into-gardening-vh7ccb32v|title=Rick Astley interview: the 1980s pop star on DIY disasters and why he's never gonna get into gardening|first=Hugh|last=Graham|date=21 October 2018|website=[[The Times]]|access-date=27 April 2019|archive-date=24 October 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181024040521/https://www.thetimes.co.uk/article/rick-astley-interview-the-1980s-pop-star-on-diy-disasters-and-why-hes-never-gonna-get-into-gardening-vh7ccb32v|url-status=live|url-access=limited}}</ref> He maintains a home recording studio and has a collection of guitars.<ref name="savage"/> Some of Astley’s personal interests include building sheds and travelling via car, once stating that he drove from New York to Los Angeles just for the fun of it.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Siwak |first=Miranda |date=2022-02-12 |title=Rick Astley: 25 Things You Don't Know About Me |url=https://www.usmagazine.com/celebrity-news/news/rick-astley-25-things-you-dont-know-about-me/ |access-date=2025-08-30 |website=Us Weekly |language=en-US}}</ref>

In 2017, Astley partnered with [[Mikkeller]], a [[Danes|Danish]] brewery, opening two pubs over the following years in [[Islington]] and [[Shoreditch]], [[London]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2023-03-17 |title=Rick Astley's Craft Beer Bar Is Never Gonna Let You Down - Jk, It Might |url=https://www.mashed.com/1230241/rick-astleys-craft-beer-bar-is-never-gonna-let-you-down-jk-it-might/ |access-date=2023-06-16 |website=Mashed |language=en-US |archive-date=16 June 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230616142653/https://www.mashed.com/1230241/rick-astleys-craft-beer-bar-is-never-gonna-let-you-down-jk-it-might/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Hind |first=John |date=2018-09-15 |title=Rick Astley: tea boy, pop star, beer hipster |language=en-GB |work=The Guardian |url=https://www.theguardian.com/food/2018/sep/15/rick-astley-tea-boy-pop-star-beer-hipster |access-date=2023-06-16 |issn=0261-3077 |archive-date=21 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240521131040/https://www.theguardian.com/food/2018/sep/15/rick-astley-tea-boy-pop-star-beer-hipster |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2022-04-30 |title=A crawl around the best celebrity-owned pubs of London |url=https://faroutmagazine.co.uk/the-best-celebrity-owned-pubs-london/ |access-date=2023-06-16 |website=Faroutmagazine.co.uk |language=en-US |archive-date=21 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240521130934/https://faroutmagazine.co.uk/the-best-celebrity-owned-pubs-london/ |url-status=live }}</ref> In January 2023, Astley sued [[Yung Gravy]] over vocal imitations on Gravy's song "[[Betty (Get Money)]]". Astley said that it was a violation of his "right of publicity",<ref>{{Cite news |last=D'Souza |first=Shaad |date=2023-01-27 |title=Yung Gravy sued by Rick Astley for 'theft of voice' |language=en-GB |work=The Guardian |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2023/jan/27/yung-gravy-sued-by-rick-astley-signature-voice-betty-get-money-never-gonna-give-you-up |access-date=2023-05-10 |issn=0261-3077 |archive-date=21 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240521130936/https://www.theguardian.com/music/2023/jan/27/yung-gravy-sued-by-rick-astley-signature-voice-betty-get-money-never-gonna-give-you-up |url-status=live }}</ref> and the case was settled in September 2023.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://deadline.com/2023/09/rick-astley-sues-rapper-yung-gravy-over-voice-imitation-1235242253/|title=Rick Astley Settles Suit Against Rapper Yung Gravy Over Voice Imitation – Update|date=27 September 2023|work=Deadline|first=Bruce|last=Haring|access-date=29 September 2023|archive-date=28 September 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230928232754/https://deadline.com/2023/09/rick-astley-sues-rapper-yung-gravy-over-voice-imitation-1235242253/|url-status=live}}</ref> Also in 2023, Astley said on ''[[This Morning (TV programme)|This Morning]]'' that he is experiencing [[hearing loss]] and now wears [[hearing aid]]s.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://journalstar.com/singer-rick-astley-reveals-hearing-loss/article_2b01e88c-90f1-532f-999b-2fee260a3002.html|title=Singer Rick Astley reveals hearing loss|work=[[Lincoln Journal Star]]|date=27 October 2023|access-date=27 October 2023|archive-date=27 October 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231027192912/https://journalstar.com/singer-rick-astley-reveals-hearing-loss/article_2b01e88c-90f1-532f-999b-2fee260a3002.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

